/**
 * @defgroup analysis Analysis
 * @brief Measurement rather than production: envelopes, spectra, pitch and onsets.
 *
 * Split sharply by realtime safety. The envelope followers and the octave-band
 * meter are fixed-size and allocation-free. Everything transform-based here
 * allocates, and the spectrograms move the work to a worker thread through a
 * lock-free queue rather than pretending otherwise.
 *
 * The transforms in this group are correctness-first references, computed in
 * double where it helps. Realtime spectral processing lives in @ref spectral
 * and goes through pffft.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup audio Audio
 * @brief Buffers, block-size adaptation and gain ramps.
 *
 * The block-size convention of the library starts here: processors are written
 * against a compile-time block size, and AudioBuffer stores frames interleaved
 * so a frame's channels share a cache line.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup audiofile AudioFile
 * @brief WAV and Ogg Vorbis reading and writing.
 *
 * Every entry point blocks and allocates. Nothing in this group belongs on an
 * audio thread.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup blockprocessors BlockProcessors
 * @brief Thin dispatch layers over per-sample loops.
 *
 * A block processor adds no arithmetic of its own; it changes the granularity
 * at which a per-sample loop is invoked, so the loop bound becomes a
 * compile-time constant and the body stays vectorisable.
 *
 * Virtual dispatch happens once per block rather than once per sample, which is
 * what makes a runtime-configurable processing chain affordable.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup delays Delays
 * @brief Delay lines, and four different answers to changing their length.
 *
 * Every delay line faces the same problem: moving a read head discontinuously
 * clicks. The classes here differ mainly in which artifact they accept instead.
 *
 * | Approach | Artifact | Class |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | Jump the read head | Click | NaiveDelay |
 * | Crossfade two heads | Brief comb filtering | DispersionDelay |
 * | Ease the read rate | Transient pitch bend | FracReadHead |
 * | Glide at 0.5x or 2x | Audible octave sweep | ModulatingDelayPitchedAdjust |
 *
 * VariSpeedTapeDelay sidesteps the question by varying the rate at which
 * material is recorded, so its buffer holds tape rather than samples.
 *
 * Interpolated lines carry wrap padding, typically six samples, so a four-point
 * interpolator can read across the seam without a branch.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup diffuser Diffuser
 * @brief Allpass chains that smear an impulse into diffusion without colouring it.
 *
 * An allpass section has a flat magnitude response and a dense, decaying
 * impulse response, which is exactly what is wanted to build echo density
 * without imposing a spectrum. Sections in series multiply density, so a short
 * chain reaches what one section could only approach at a gain high enough to
 * ring audibly.
 *
 * Lengths are snapped to distinct primes throughout, so no two sections share a
 * period and reinforce each other into a pitch.
 *
 * @see https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/Schroeder_Allpass_Sections.html
 */

/**
 * @defgroup dynamics Dynamics
 * @brief Level-dependent gain control (compression, limiting).
 */

/**
 * @defgroup filters Filters
 * @brief One-pole, biquad, state variable, ladder and windowed-sinc filters.
 *
 * Transposed Direct Form II throughout the biquads: two state words, and the
 * form usually preferred for floating point, where Direct Form I is the safer
 * choice in fixed point.
 *
 * Two coefficient conventions coexist and are worth telling apart. The
 * lowpass, highpass, bandpass, notch and peak designs use the prewarped
 * K = tan(pi*f/fs); the allpass and shelf designs use the cookbook
 * alpha = sin(w0)/(2Q). Both are bilinear designs; only the algebra differs.
 *
 * SvfResoBP is the exception to the direct forms. It integrates the analog
 * state variable filter by the trapezoidal rule and resolves the zero-delay
 * feedback algebraically, which is what keeps it stable when the cutoff is
 * modulated per sample.
 *
 * @see https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/filters/
 * @see https://webaudio.github.io/Audio-EQ-Cookbook/audio-eq-cookbook.html
 */

/**
 * @defgroup generators Generators
 * @brief Sources: modal voices, clicks, noise processes and musical clocks.
 *
 * The modal voices ring resonators rather than summing oscillators, so each
 * partial carries its own decay without an envelope per partial, and partials
 * enter on staggered delays because a real resonator does not excite every mode
 * at once.
 *
 * The noise sources are deliberately not white. Mean reversion, velvet grids
 * and fractal walks each produce a different kind of structured irregularity,
 * which is what makes them usable as modulation rather than as hiss.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup helpers Helpers
 * @brief Small utilities: array construction, SIMD selection, test formatting.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup modulation Modulation
 * @brief Wow, flutter and the position modulators that drive delay read heads.
 *
 * Pitch deviation follows the rate of change of the delay, not the delay
 * itself. A triangle sweep therefore has a piecewise-constant derivative and
 * produces a two-state warble rather than vibrato, which is why SineModulation
 * exists alongside Modulation and is the default for anything audible.
 *
 * Wow and flutter are the slow and fast halves of tape speed error, split at
 * roughly 6 Hz. Wow is driven by a mean-reverting random process rather than an
 * oscillator, because a listener picks a repeating cycle out immediately.
 *
 * @see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wow_and_flutter
 */

/**
 * @defgroup naivegenerators NaiveGenerators
 * @brief Waveforms computed straight from the phase, aliasing included.
 *
 * The aliasing is the point. These define what the ideal waveform is before
 * band limiting, and are the baseline the wavetable oscillator is measured
 * against. Sine and noise do not alias and are usable directly.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup nonlinear NonLinear
 * @brief Memoryless and hysteretic nonlinearities.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup numbers Numbers
 * @brief Interpolation, conversions, easing curves, primes and approximations.
 *
 * The interpolators are the substrate of every fractional delay in the library.
 * None checks bounds: the caller guarantees the support width, which is why the
 * delay lines carry wrap padding.
 *
 * The prime helpers exist for one reason. Delay lengths sharing a common factor
 * reinforce at that period and turn a diffuse tail into an audible pitch;
 * mutually prime lengths have no common period short enough to matter.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup parameters Parameters
 * @brief Smoothing and mapping between control values and DSP quantities.
 *
 * Applying a control change at once puts a step into the signal: a click on a
 * gain, a zipper on anything swept. Everything here exists to spread that step
 * over a few milliseconds.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup reverbs Reverbs
 * @brief Feedback delay networks and the Hadamard mixing they are built on.
 *
 * An FDN needs an orthogonal mixing matrix, so that the feedback loop neither
 * creates nor destroys energy on its own. With the mixing lossless, decay time
 * becomes a single scalar gain and nothing else has to be retuned when it
 * changes.
 *
 * Hadamard matrices are the cheapest orthogonal choice: every entry is +/-1, so
 * mixing is adds and subtracts with no multiply. The mixing functions here are
 * unnormalised, and the tanks fold the 1/sqrt(order) factor into their feedback
 * gain instead.
 *
 * The `HadamardWalsh*` files compute the identical matrix in identical row
 * order via the fast butterfly, not a sequency-ordered variant: 160 adds at
 * order 32 against 1024 written flat.
 *
 * FdnTankRef is the bare network, kept as the baseline the elaborated tanks are
 * measured against.
 *
 * @see https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/pasp/FDN_Reverberation.html
 */

/**
 * @defgroup sampler Sampler
 * @brief Loop capture, slicing, sample playback and step sequencing.
 *
 * The realtime and file-IO halves are strictly separated. Players and
 * recorders reserve all storage at construction and borrow audio by span or
 * pointer; the file classes block and allocate freely.
 *
 * The sequencer is driven from the same grid-event stream as the looper's own
 * clock rather than from a clock of its own, which makes drift against the loop
 * impossible rather than merely unlikely.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup sequencing Sequencing
 * @brief BPM-invariant loop position and time-signature timeline keeping.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup srconverter SamplerateConverter
 * @brief Push and pull sample rate conversion over windowed-sinc kernels.
 *
 * Push and pull differ in who drives. The push converter takes input when the
 * caller has it and returns whatever comes out, which suits a live audio path;
 * the pull converter asks a callback for input as it needs it, which suits a
 * file or generator.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup spectral Spectral
 * @brief Realtime frequency-domain processing over pffft.
 *
 * @see https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/sasp/
 */

/**
 * @defgroup synthhandling SynthHandling
 * @brief MIDI-side state that is not signal processing.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup wavetables Wavetables
 * @brief Mipmapped band-limited wavetable oscillators.
 *
 * A single table holds a fixed set of harmonics, so playing it faster pushes
 * those harmonics past Nyquist. Each mipmap level therefore drops the partials
 * that would alias at its own pitch, and lookup takes the highest level still
 * safe for the requested increment.
 *
 * @see https://www.earlevel.com/main/2012/05/04/a-wavetable-oscillator-part-1/
 */
